      explanation: "**Choice A is correct.** The passage lines up the shower's dates with Earth's crossing of a comet's debris stream, so the safe conclusion is that the meteors are grains from that stream burning up.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- How to spot it: an inference question wants the conclusion the passage forces, not a leap beyond it. Follow the chain of facts and pick the choice that simply finishes it.\n- The particles are no larger than sand grains and \"arrive on the same dates annually.\"\n- Astronomers matched that timing to a comet that \"sheds dust each time it passes near the Sun, leaving a long stream of debris strung out along its path.\"\n- Earth crosses that stream \"at the same point in its own orbit every August,\" so the grains we see burning are the comet's shed dust.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- B: Nothing in the passage suggests the particles come from Earth; they are traced to a comet.\n- C: The passage gives no end date for the shower, and the comet keeps shedding dust on every pass.\n- D: A single repeating stream is the whole point, so a new source each year contradicts the text.",
      passage: "Each year in mid-August, observers see a burst of meteors that all appear to streak away from the same patch of sky. The particles that produce them are no larger than sand grains, and they arrive on the same dates annually. Astronomers have matched that timing to the orbit of a comet that sheds dust every time it passes near the Sun, leaving a long stream of debris strung out along its path. Earth crosses that stream at the same point in its own orbit every August, which suggests that the meteors ______",
      _meta: {
        anchor: "meteor showers: annual streams from comet debris along a shared orbit"
      },
