      explanation: "**Choice C is correct.** The passage makes a marsh's rise depend on incoming silt, so two marshes facing the same sea-level rise can still end up differently depending on how much river sediment each receives.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- How to spot it: find the one variable the passage says changes the outcome, then let the blank turn on that variable. Here the variable is the sediment supply, not the rate of sea-level rise.\n- A marsh builds upward because the tide carries in silt and the grasses \"slow the water enough that the particles settle among their stems.\"\n- Where sediment is plentiful, a marsh \"can gain height as fast as the water rises, or faster.\"\n- But the silt comes from rivers, and where dams and channel work \"have cut off that supply, the grasses trap far less.\"\n- So identical sea-level rise does not guarantee identical outcomes - the sediment each marsh still gets decides whether it keeps up.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- A: Twice-daily tides reach both marshes, yet the passage says what matters is how much silt those tides carry.\n- B: Grass species is never the variable the passage points to; sediment supply is.\n- D: The passage explains height gained from settling silt, not height lost to decaying grass.",
      passage: "A salt marsh does not simply drown as the sea rises. Twice a day the tide carries in silt, and the marsh grasses slow the water enough that the particles settle among their stems, so the surface builds upward year by year. Where sediment is plentiful, a marsh can gain height as fast as the water rises, or faster. But the silt arrives from rivers, and where dams and channel work have cut off that supply, the grasses trap far less. Researchers therefore expect that two marshes facing identical rates of sea-level rise ______",
