      explanation: "**Choice C is correct.** The word doing the action is the single \"layer,\" so it takes the singular verb \"keeps.\"\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- How to spot it: find the one word the verb is really about and ignore everything stuffed in between. Ask \"what keeps the layers from shifting?\" The answer is the layer of batting - just one.\n- The subject is \"layer.\" The plural words before the blank - \"rows,\" \"stitches\" - sit inside an \"along with\" phrase, which never changes the subject's number.\n- A singular subject takes a singular verb, so \"the thin layer ... keeps the three layers from shifting\" is correct. This match is called subject-verb agreement.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- A: \"Keep\" is the plural form, matching the nearby plural words instead of the real subject.\n- B: \"Are keeping\" is also plural, so it agrees with the wrong words.\n- D: \"Have kept\" uses the plural \"have\" where singular \"layer\" needs \"keeps.\"",
